WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.837
Run this query and compare several truncated versions of each order timestamp.

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:08.557
The original timestamp keeps the precise event time.

00:00:08.557 --> 00:00:11.502
The day version maps it to the start of the day.

00:00:11.502 --> 00:00:14.577
The month version maps it to the start of the month.

00:00:14.577 --> 00:00:22.042
This is how a precise event can be analyzed at a coarser reporting grain without changing the stored

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:27.302
source value. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:27.302 --> 00:00:33.347
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.347 --> 00:00:35.857
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:41.262
In a real setting such as a hospital calculating length of stay and follow-up dates,

00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:47.222
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.222 --> 00:00:49.522
before discussing syntax.

00:00:49.522 --> 00:00:55.760
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
